Sure! Below is a simple example of a “card” that rotates around its Y‑axis when you hover over it.  
It uses only **HTML** and **CSS** (no JavaScript).  

---

## 1️⃣ Markup (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Rotating Card Demo</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>

<div class="card-wrapper">
    <div class="card">
        <!-- Front side -->
        <div class="card-face front">
            <h2>Front Side</h2>
            <p>This is the front of the card.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Back side -->
        <div class="card-face back">
            <h2>Back Side</h2>
            <p>This is the back of the card.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

* `card-wrapper` keeps the perspective so the rotation looks 3D.  
* Inside we have two faces (`front` and `back`) that will be swapped on hover.

---

## 2️⃣ Styling (CSS)

Create a file called `styles.css` (or put the styles inside a `<style>` tag).

```css
/* Basic reset */
* { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

/* Center the card on the page */
body {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    height: 100vh;
    background: #f0f0f0;
}

/* Wrapper gives perspective to the 3D transform */
.card-wrapper {
    width: 300px;
    height: 200px;
    perspective: 800px;          /* How far the viewer is from the card */
}

/* The card itself */
.card {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    position: relative;
    transition: transform 0.8s ease-in-out;   /* Smooth animation */
    transform-style: preserve-3d;             /* Keep children in 3D space */
}

/* Hover effect – rotate 180° around Y axis */
.card:hover {
    transform: rotateY(180deg);
}

/* Common face styling */
.card-face {
    position: absolute;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    backface-visibility: hidden;   /* Hide the backside when flipped */
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 10px;
}

/* Front face */
.front {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ff7e5f, #feb47b); /* Warm gradient */
}

/* Back face – rotated 180° so it shows after flip */
.back {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #43cea2, #185a9d); /* Cool gradient */
    transform: rotateY(180deg);
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Element | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `.card-wrapper` | Provides a 3D perspective. |
| `.card` | Holds both faces and performs the rotation. |
| `.card-face` | Each side of the card. |
| `backface-visibility: hidden` | Hides the side that should not be visible during the flip. |
| `transform: rotateY(180deg)` on `.back` | Positions the back side behind the front initially. |
| `.card:hover` | Rotates the whole card when hovered. |

---

## 3️⃣ Result

When you open the HTML file in a browser, you’ll see a card centered on the screen. Hover your mouse over it and watch it flip around the Y‑axis, revealing the other side.

Feel free to tweak the dimensions, colors, or transition timing to fit your design. Happy coding!
